Scott defines the finite set of elements by \(\{x_0,\ldots,x_{n-1}\}=\{z\in\mathbb{P}D\mid \exists X_i\in x_i.\, \bigcup_{i<n}(\uparrow X_i)\subseteq z\}\), and notes that the same condition may be written \(\forall i<n.\, X_i\in z\). `PDmemFinJoin` requires representatives \(X_i\in x_i\) such that `W` is a power-domain neighbourhood and `upSet (X i) ⊆ W` for every `i`. The set `upSet X` is `{Y | Y ⊆ X}`, Definition 7.9’s down-set \(\downarrow X\), so the formal inclusion is \(\downarrow X_i\subseteq W\). For a downward-closed power-domain neighbourhood that is equivalent to \(X_i\in W\), which is the parenthetical form, whereas containment of the principal filter of supersets is not. `PDmem_finJoin_iUnion` turns the pointwise inclusions, for a nonempty family, into one inclusion of the indexed union, and conversely. `PDfinJoin` returns `PDfinJoinZero`, defined as `PowerDomain.bot`, when `n = 0`, and `PDfinJoinSucc` otherwise; `PDmem_finJoin` splits on that clause, and `PDmem_finJoinZero` says the empty join contains a neighbourhood exactly when the neighbourhood is the master. The displayed comprehension at \(n=0\) has an empty union, which is contained in every power-domain neighbourhood, so the set of such neighbourhoods is all of \(\mathbb{P}D\), not the bottom filter. `PDfinJoinSucc` proves the filter laws for the nonempty case: the master by choosing `V.master` in every coordinate, using that each element contains the master and each down-set of the master sits inside the power-domain master; binary intersections by intersecting the representatives, invoking `upSet_inter`, and using closure of each element and of the power domain under intersection; upward closure by retaining the same representatives. `PDsingleton` is the join of the constant length-one family, and `PDmem_singleton` unfolds it to a single representative. **Theorem:** partial — a nonempty join is the filter of power-domain neighbourhoods containing a finite union of down-sets, matching the parenthetical membership reading, while the printed up-arrows are interpreted as down-sets and the empty join is the bottom element rather than the set of every power-domain neighbourhood. **Proof:** partial — the successor filter laws and `PDmem_finJoin_iUnion` establish the nonempty union form, and the zero case is the rewrite of bottom membership to the master rather than the empty-union case of the comprehension.
